    "content": "#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"\nGenerate 51 perturbed diamond C structures from the primitive cell.\n\nStructure 000 is the unperturbed base structure.\nStructures 001-050 are perturbed copies with:\n  - Random atomic displacements (Gaussian, sigma = 0.05 A)\n  - Small random lattice strain (max 1%)\n\nDisplacement justification:\n  At room temperature (300 K), the mean-square displacement of C in diamond\n  is approximately 0.001 A^2 (from Debye-Wilson analysis of X-ray/neutron data),\n  giving a standard deviation of ~0.03 A. We use sigma = 0.05 A to be slightly\n  conservative and ensure the perturbations are chemically reasonable (no bond\n  breaking, no unphysical overlaps) while still sampling a meaningful range\n  of thermal configurations.\n\nLattice strain justification:\n  A 1% random strain is small enough to preserve the diamond structure's\n  integrity while sampling the effect of slight lattice distortion on the\n  electronic structure.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport numpy as np\nfrom ase.io import read, write\nfrom ase import Atoms\n\n# Read the primitive cell (already reduced in STEP 1)\nbase = read('structures/primitive_C.cif')\nprint(f\"Base structure: {len(base)} atoms, {len(set(base.get_chemical_symbols()))} element types\")\n\n# Set random seed for reproducibility\nnp.random.seed(42)\n\n# Displacement parameters\nsigma_displacement = 0.05  # Angstrom, see justification above\nmax_strain = 0.01  # 1% maximum strain\n\n# Write structure 000 (unperturbed)\nwrite('structures/structure_000.cif', base)\nprint(f\"Wrote structures/structure_000.cif (unperturbed)\")\n\n# Generate perturbed structures 001-050\nfor i in range(1, 51):\n    # Make a copy of the base structure\n    atoms = base.copy()\n    \n    # Apply random atomic displacements (Gaussian, in Cartesian coordinates)\n    # Displacements are in Angstrom, applied to Cartesian positions\n    displacements = np.random.normal(0, sigma_displacement, size=atoms.positions.shape)\n    atoms.positions += displacements\n    \n    # Apply small random lattice strain\n    # Generate a random strain tensor (symmetric, traceless for volume preservation)\n    strain = np.random.uniform(-max_strain, max_strain, size=(3, 3))\n    # Make it symmetric\n    strain = 0.5 * (strain + strain.T)\n    # Make it traceless (preserve volume)\n    strain -= np.trace(strain) / 3.0 * np.eye(3)\n    \n    # Apply strain to the cell\n    # new_cell = (I + strain) @ old_cell\n    strain_matrix = np.eye(3) + strain\n    atoms.cell = strain_matrix @ atoms.cell\n    \n    # Wrap positions back into the cell (fractional coordinates in [0, 1))\n    atoms.wrap()\n    \n    # INVARIANT 1: Assert nat/ntyp are consistent with the actual structure\n    nat = len(atoms)\n    ntyp = len(set(atoms.get_chemical_symbols()))\n    assert nat == 2, f\"Structure {i:03d}: expected 2 atoms, got {nat}\"\n    assert ntyp == 1, f\"Structure {i:03d}: expected 1 element type, got {ntyp}\"\n    \n    # Write the perturbed structure\n    filename = f'structures/structure_{i:03d}.cif'\n    write(filename, atoms)\n    print(f\"Wrote {filename} (perturbed)\")\n\nprint(f\"\\nDone. Generated 51 structures (000-050).\")\n"
  }
}
